"""Reference-based narrative sections for the revised project report."""

def introduction(doc,p,h,page):
    doc.add_heading('1 Introduction',1)
    h('1.1 Background')
    p('Federated learning enables clients to train on distributed datasets while exchanging model information instead of collecting all raw samples at a central location {ref:mcmahan}. In a decentralized system, neighboring devices exchange updates directly. This architecture is relevant to edge and IoT deployments where a central aggregation service may be unavailable or costly to maintain. Keeping data local is a data-handling property; a formal privacy guarantee requires an additional mechanism.')
    p('Network structure determines which clients can exchange information, while unreliable delivery determines which of those exchanges actually influence a model. The resulting learning behavior must be evaluated together with the distribution of data and the amount of local computation. A high final test accuracy alone cannot isolate the contribution of communication.')
    page('1 Introduction continued')
    h('1.2 State of the Art')
    p('Decentralized SGD and decentralized federated averaging establish server-free training through local optimization and neighbor exchange {ref:r3}{ref:r27}. Consensus-based IoT learning and peer-to-peer graph learning further demonstrate that cooperation can be organized without a central model aggregator {ref:r7}{ref:r26}. These approaches motivate our implementation, while their update rules and convergence assumptions remain specific to each method.')
    p('Unreliable communication is already a substantial research topic. Soft-DSGD uses partially received information and reliability-aware mixing; D2D learning studies address unreliable wireless exchange; and Li et al. analyze aggregation bias and the number of local aggregations under imperfect channels {ref:r10}{ref:r11}{ref:r14}. Communication-efficient methods also include compression, sparse exchange, and adaptive communication rather than only whole-message suppression {ref:r5}{ref:r13}.')
    h('1.3 The Research Gap')
    p('The practical question addressed here is how much a specified gossip implementation gains over matched local-only training when data heterogeneity, communication suppression, and channel impairments are varied within a common evaluation protocol. We investigate how that gain relates to actual payload traffic and model disagreement, and where ideal spectral predictions cease to describe the complete learning procedure. Section 2.4 develops this evaluation gap from the selected literature.')
    h('1.4 Objectives')
    for text in [
        '1. Measure the accuracy gain of communication using matched local-only controls on IID and non-IID data.',
        '2. Evaluate independent erasures, burst losses, and finite-round delayed delivery with explicit message accounting.',
        '3. Quantify the accuracy-traffic tradeoff of fixed event thresholds using directed-message and payload-byte counts.',
        '4. Compare Ring, Grid, and Random graph instances, and test whether small-network conclusions persist as client count grows.',
        '5. Verify ideal spectral averaging calculations separately from empirical neural-network training.']:
        p(text)
    h('1.5 Contributions')
    p('M1 provides a reproducible local-SGD and neighbor-averaging simulator with version-aware delayed delivery and measured aggregation-induced mean drift. M2 adds sender-side event triggering while sharing the M1 receiver path. M3 provides signed spectra, SLEM, and a squared-disagreement bound for ideal fixed averaging. The empirical contribution is a 155-run, five-seed evaluation with local-only controls, heterogeneous data, channel comparisons, larger networks, and a restricted CIFAR-10 extension. The study characterizes this implementation rather than introducing a new convergence theorem.')


def literature(p,h,page,table,caption):
    page('2 Literature Review')
    h('2.1 Decentralized Federated Learning and Gossip Based Optimization')
    p('Distributed averaging provides the mathematical basis for exchanging information over a graph. Xiao and Boyd study linear iterations for computing network averages, while Boyd et al. analyze randomized gossip and its relationship to matrix spectra {ref:r1}{ref:r2}. These foundations concern specified averaging operators. Applying them to a learning system requires attention to the local optimization steps and the communication mechanism used between those steps.')
    p('Lian et al. analyze decentralized parallel SGD, and Sun et al. study decentralized federated averaging with momentum and a quantized variant {ref:r3}{ref:r27}. Both connect local computation to neighbor communication, but an implementation using retained local optimizer momentum and receiver-wise normalization should not automatically inherit their theoretical guarantees. Our experimental protocol therefore records its exact update policy and assesses learning empirically.')
    p('Hegedűs et al. compare gossip learning with federated learning, including communication cost and differing data distributions. Savazzi et al. develop consensus-based cooperation for IoT devices, while Lalitha et al. propose graph-based peer-to-peer learning with a Bayesian-like belief update {ref:r6}{ref:r7}{ref:r26}. These studies establish several ways of organizing collaboration; their algorithms are not interchangeable implementations of one common averaging rule.')
    p('Gao et al. discuss the connection between decentralized optimization and federated learning, and Liu et al. examine how multiple local updates and communication steps balance computing cost and model consensus {ref:r28}{ref:r22}. This motivates specifying training and communication budgets separately. Our core comparison fixes ten rounds and one local epoch, and a separate IID control repeats the original twenty-round, two-local-epoch budget.')
    h('Implication for this study')
    p('A credible comparison needs a no-communication control, a precise local-update policy, and a clear definition of the quantity being mixed. We consequently compare individual client models under matched partitions and starting weights, retain optimizer momentum locally, and distinguish prediction accuracy from parameter agreement.')

    page('2.2 Communication Efficiency and Learning over Imperfect Channels')
    p('Communication can be reduced by sending smaller updates, selecting different peers, or suppressing an entire transmission opportunity. Koloskova et al. analyze compressed decentralized optimization. GossipFL combines model sparsification with adaptive communication and peer selection {ref:r5}{ref:r13}. Our M2 trigger instead suppresses a complete float32 model broadcast. Compression distortion and message erasure are distinct mechanisms, so results from one mechanism are not treated as direct evidence for the other.')
    p('Wireless learning additionally depends on how updates are transmitted. Xing et al. analyze digital and analog decentralized learning over D2D networks, including channel effects under stated assumptions. Chen et al. review distributed learning in wireless networks more broadly {ref:r8}{ref:r9}. The present simulator operates at the application-message level: it tests erasures and stale arrivals, and does not implement an analog waveform or a complete physical-layer channel.')
    p('The closest unreliable-link studies are Ye et al., Jiang et al., and Li et al. {ref:r10}{ref:r11}{ref:r14}. Ye et al. optimize mixing using link reliability information, while Li et al. relate imperfect aggregation to model bias and aggregation frequency. Our receiver-normalized baseline does not reproduce those algorithms. It provides a controlled reference whose average drift and finite-budget behavior can be measured explicitly.')
    p('Centralized and cellular FL studies provide complementary perspectives. Yang et al. study scheduling policies, Salehi and Hossain consider unreliable resource-constrained cellular networks, and Li et al. consider fairness with unreliable IoT links {ref:r15}{ref:r16}{ref:r19}. These topics motivate reporting actual communication and client-level performance, although centralized scheduling and fairness optimization are outside our implemented protocol.')
    p('Over-the-air and noisy-channel studies also require separate assumptions. Xu et al. examine learning-rate optimization for over-the-air computation; Amiri et al. analyze a noisy downlink; and Wei and Shen study learning over noisy channels {ref:r17}{ref:r18}{ref:r20}. Their continuous-valued channel errors are not equivalent to independently erasing complete model messages. We use them to delimit the wireless setting rather than to claim that our erasure model validates their analyses.')

    page('2.3 Network Topology Consensus and Convergence')
    p('Nedić et al. explain how graph structure and communication assumptions affect decentralized optimization {ref:r4}. The ideal mixing matrix is therefore a useful design descriptor, but its spectral gap is not a universal predictor of final neural-network accuracy. Local gradients, heterogeneous data, directed missing messages, and stale parameters can change the relation between an ideal graph property and observed model disagreement.')
    p('The surveys by Martínez Beltrán et al. and Yuan et al. organize DFL through architectures, communication mechanisms, topologies, system designs, and open challenges {ref:r23}{ref:r24}. Chung et al. specifically review non-IID DFL, covering architecture, topology, optimization, security, and evaluation practices {ref:r25}. Together these sources motivate an evaluation that states its data distribution and system assumptions instead of treating IID accuracy as a complete assessment.')
    p('Related studies also define important boundaries. Chen et al. develop a differentially private wireless DFL scheme; Imteaj et al. survey resource-constrained IoT learning; Nguyen et al. study self-organizing distributed learning; and Chen et al. investigate mobility-aware vehicular DFL {ref:r12}{ref:r21}{ref:r29}{ref:r30}. Our static-graph simulator does not implement privacy protection, autonomous network organization, or vehicle mobility. Those are extensions rather than capabilities inferred from keeping samples local.')
    table(['Research strand','Representative references','Role in the present study'],[
        ['Optimization and averaging','{ref:r1}{ref:r2}{ref:r3}{ref:r4}{ref:r27}','Defines ideal mixing and local-update foundations; assumptions are stated explicitly.'],
        ['Unreliable decentralized exchange','{ref:r8}{ref:r10}{ref:r11}{ref:r14}','Motivates loss, stale-arrival, and average-drift measurements.'],
        ['Communication efficiency','{ref:r5}{ref:r13}{ref:r22}','Distinguishes whole-message suppression from compression and adaptive peer selection.'],
        ['Heterogeneity and system evaluation','{ref:r23}{ref:r24}{ref:r25}','Motivates explicit non-IID settings and consistent evaluation metrics.'],
        ['Privacy resources and mobility','{ref:r12}{ref:r19}{ref:r21}{ref:r29}{ref:r30}','Identifies capabilities that remain outside the implemented simulator.']],[1.6,1.65,3.71])
    caption('Table 1. Literature synthesis. The table classifies the selected studies by their main relevance; it is not a claim that each paper studies only one issue.')

    page('2.4 Research Gap and Positioning of the Present Study')
    p('The selected literature already establishes decentralized optimization, wireless D2D learning, communication-efficient gossip, and learning over imperfect channels. Our research gap is therefore framed as a controlled evaluation question: for the specified receiver-normalized gossip procedure, how do the value and cost of communication change when data heterogeneity, graph structure, and message delivery are varied under matched training conditions?')
    p('First, a high accuracy on an easy IID task does not identify how much collaboration contributes. We address this measurement gap with paired local-only controls, exactly two-label client partitions, and Dirichlet α=0.1 partitions. Second, an implemented delay buffer does not establish tolerance to stale information. We evaluate finite delays and burst losses while distinguishing arrived, dropped, and pending messages. Third, suppressing a client broadcast is not the same as saving one neighbor message on an irregular graph. We therefore account for actual directed attempts and payload bytes.')
    p('A fourth question concerns the interpretation of topology. Ideal averaging theory characterizes a fixed operator, whereas learning repeatedly adds local updates. We test the ideal scalar bound separately and compare trained models at several client counts, without asserting that a larger ideal spectral gap guarantees better accuracy. This links a well-defined mathematical check to an empirical learning evaluation without treating the two as the same result.')
    table(['Evaluation question','Literature basis','Evidence provided here'],[
        ['When does communication improve learning?','Peer-to-peer learning, decentralized averaging, and non-IID DFL {ref:r26}{ref:r27}{ref:r25}','Matched local-only controls; IID, two-class, and Dirichlet comparisons; paired five-seed gains.'],
        ['How do losses and stale arrivals change outcomes?','Unreliable-link DFL and imperfect aggregation {ref:r10}{ref:r11}{ref:r14}','Bernoulli and Gilbert-Elliott erasures; maximum delay 0, 1, 2, 4; arrival accounting and mean drift.'],
        ['What does an event trigger actually save?','Compressed, sparse, adaptive, and budget-aware communication {ref:r5}{ref:r13}{ref:r22}','Fixed-threshold accuracy-traffic curves; broadcasts, directed messages, and bytes counted separately.'],
        ['Which spectral conclusions transfer to training?','Distributed averaging and topology-dependent optimization {ref:r1}{ref:r2}{ref:r4}','Signed spectra and ideal squared-error bounds; separate N=5/20/50 learning comparisons.']],[1.65,2.1,3.21])
    caption('Table 2. Research questions derived from the literature and the corresponding experimental evidence. These are questions evaluated by this project, not assertions that all earlier work omitted them.')
    p('The contribution is the combined reproducible measurement protocol and its evidence for this implementation. Comparisons cover 31 conditions with five seeds each. The CIFAR-10 extension is limited to local-only versus Ring learning, and the scale study compares Ring and Grid with fixed total data. We do not benchmark against reimplementations of Soft-DSGD, GossipFL, or every method in the bibliography; the results consequently do not establish superiority over those algorithms.')


def approach(p,h,page):
    page('3.3 Proposed Approach')
    p('Figure 1 summarizes the workflow. Local-only training is the control, M1 exchanges after each round of local training, and M2 adds a sender decision before the same receiver update. M3 runs separately on the ideal graph matrix. Every comparison uses the same partition and initialization within a seed.')
    h('M1 Standard Gossip Federated Learning')
    p('Every client trains locally, snapshots its parameters, sends the snapshot to each neighbor, and applies Equation (4) to the newest acceptable arrivals. All models are snapshotted before any receiver is updated, preventing an in-place update order from changing the meaning of a synchronous communication round. The no-communication control performs the same local training and evaluation while making no channel calls.')
    h('M2 Event Triggered Gossip')
    p('Each sender compares its post-training model with its last-transmitted snapshot using Equation (5). A triggered client attempts one directed model message per neighbor; a silent client may still receive messages. Threshold zero always sends. Standard and event-triggered methods share the receiver implementation, and reproducibility is checked by exact parameter hashes at zero threshold.')
    h('M3 Spectral Gap Analysis')
    p('The graph matrix is analyzed for signed eigenvalues, SLEM, spectral gap, and a sufficient ideal averaging time. A scalar simulation checks the squared-disagreement bound under the same fixed matrix {ref:r1}{ref:r2}. Neural-network training is evaluated empirically, with local SGD, missing messages, and stale arrivals outside that fixed-matrix proof.')
    h('Sequence of Operation')
    for text in [
        '1. Initialize the seed, benchmark tensors, disjoint client partitions, graph, common model weights, and channel parameters.',
        '2. Run the specified local SGD epochs independently at every client, preserving local optimizer state.',
        '3. For M2, apply the change threshold; for M1, enable all sender broadcasts.',
        '4. Sample directed-link erasures and surviving-message delays; release arrivals due in the current round.',
        '5. Reject outdated versions and normalize the available neighbor weights before updating each receiver.',
        '6. Evaluate individual client models and record mean accuracy, loss, disagreement, aggregation drift, and traffic.',
        '7. Repeat for the specified budget, save the completed run, aggregate independent seeds, and analyze the ideal graph separately.']:
        p(text)


def discussion(p,h,page,select,base,stat,summary):
    page('5 Discussion')
    h('5.1 Why Ring and Grid Performance Depends on Network Size')
    p('The five-node Ring is regular, while the trimmed five-node Grid includes a degree-one node. Their ideal gaps are approximately 0.461 and 0.211. This explains a difference in ideal mixing for those instances, not a general preference for Ring. The larger-network experiment also changes the amount of data held by each client because total data remain fixed.')
    ring5=base('two-class','gossip','ring',clients=5)[0]
    grid5=base('two-class','gossip','grid',clients=5)[0]
    ring50=base('two-class','gossip','ring',clients=50)[0]
    grid50=base('two-class','gossip','grid',clients=50)[0]
    p(f"On two-class MNIST, five-client mean accuracy is {100*ring5['final_accuracy']['mean']:.2f}% for Ring and {100*grid5['final_accuracy']['mean']:.2f}% for Grid. At fifty clients, the corresponding means are {100*ring50['final_accuracy']['mean']:.2f}% and {100*grid50['final_accuracy']['mean']:.2f}%. The observed ordering therefore depends on network size and the complete training protocol; within each size, the methods use matched data allocations.")
    h('5.2 Robustness to Packet Loss and Relation to Theory')
    p('Under Dirichlet MNIST, Ring mean accuracy decreases from 89.83% without loss to 86.39% under 20% Bernoulli erasure, and to 69.22% when maximum delay is four rounds at that erasure rate. These means characterize the tested budget and receiver policy. Confidence intervals and realized traffic are reported with the comparisons. Similar performance under one channel condition cannot establish arbitrary loss or delay tolerance.')
    p('Receiver-wise renormalization preserves row sums but can move the arithmetic network mean when receptions are asymmetric. Our measured aggregation-only drift exposes this property. Ideal spectral averaging predicts contraction for a fixed symmetric operator, whereas the learning system introduces gradients and time-dependent information. This distinction is consistent with the emphasis on communication assumptions and aggregation effects in the related literature {ref:r4}{ref:r10}{ref:r14}.')
    h('5.3 Event Triggered Communication Analysis')
    ring0=select(dataset='MNIST',partition='dirichlet',method='gossip',topology='ring',clients=5,loss=0.0,delay=0,delta=0.0)[0]
    ring2=select(dataset='MNIST',partition='dirichlet',method='event',topology='ring',clients=5,loss=0.0,delay=0,delta=2.0)[0]
    p(f"At δ=2.0, Ring saves {ring2['message_savings_pct']['mean']:.1f}% of directed messages, but mean accuracy falls from {100*ring0['final_accuracy']['mean']:.2f}% to {100*ring2['final_accuracy']['mean']:.2f}%. The practical result is an accuracy-traffic tradeoff. A threshold based on an absolute parameter norm is model- and budget-dependent. Compression and adaptive peer selection provide different design options {{ref:r5}}{{ref:r13}}, which are not implemented by simply raising this threshold.")
    p('On an irregular graph, a silent high-degree client removes more directed messages than a silent low-degree client. Broadcast suppression is consequently reported separately from traffic savings. For example, the original Grid counts contain eight suppressed broadcasts out of 100 opportunities, but 188 rather than 200 attempted neighbor messages: 8% broadcast suppression corresponds to 6% payload traffic reduction in that run.')
    page('5 Discussion continued')
    h('5.4 Limitations')
    p('Five seeds provide an initial uncertainty estimate. Reusing a benchmark test set does not replace evaluation on new populations, and no validation-driven hyperparameter search is performed. The client objective is uniformly weighted, unlike a pooled sample-weighted objective for unequal data sizes. Local momentum is retained after mixing. The core horizon is short, and larger-network comparisons fix total data rather than per-client examples.')
    p('The channel erases whole model messages and omits physical packetization, retries, measured bandwidth, energy, and real wall-clock latency. Integer-round delay is not a fully asynchronous execution model. Static, honest clients are assumed. Differential privacy, secure aggregation, Byzantine protection, dynamic vehicular topology, and average-preserving lossy aggregation remain outside the implementation {ref:r12}{ref:r29}{ref:r30}. The CIFAR-10 extension compares only local-only and Ring learning, and no claim of superiority over the referenced algorithms follows from these experiments.')
    h('5.5 Communication Benefit under the Original Training Budget')
    long=select(dataset='MNIST',partition='iid',topology='ring',clients=5,rounds=20,local_epochs=2,batch_size=64)
    local=[g for g in long if g['method']=='local'][0]
    gossip=[g for g in long if g['method']=='gossip'][0]
    matched=[r for r in summary['paired_vs_local'] if r.get('dataset')=='MNIST' and r['partition']=='iid' and r['topology']=='ring' and r.get('rounds')==20][0]['accuracy_gain_pp']
    p(f"At twenty rounds, two local epochs, and batch size 64, local-only mean accuracy is {stat(local)}%, compared with {stat(gossip)}% for Ring gossip. The paired five-seed gain is {matched['mean']:.2f} ± {matched['ci95_half']:.2f} percentage points. Thus communication makes a measurable contribution even in this IID setting, while the gain is much smaller than in the heterogeneous short-budget comparisons. These are newly matched control runs, not the historical single-seed records.")
    h('5.6 Findings in Relation to the Research Gap')
    p('The local-only controls answer whether communication contributes under a specified budget; non-IID runs make the role of shared class information visible; channel experiments exercise stale and missing information; and event-trigger results connect actual traffic to accuracy. The ideal scalar check establishes a separate mathematical property. Together these results address the evaluation questions in Section 2.4 without implying an improved algorithmic guarantee for all wireless settings.')
    p('The reviewed surveys and systems papers also identify the next boundaries to study: stronger heterogeneity, changing topology, resource constraints, privacy mechanisms, and alternative aggregation policies {ref:r21}{ref:r23}{ref:r24}{ref:r25}. A broader benchmark should reimplement selected methods under matched budgets before drawing comparative algorithm rankings.')
